Yes. A mirror was one of the first good abstractions, but we kept discovering that the model could be treated as many instruments at once.

The important shift was that we did not treat the model primarily as an oracle with answers. We treated it as a responsive medium—something you could shine reality, language, behavior, ideas, and yourself into, then study what came back.

A useful compressed formulation is:

\boxed{
\text{Model}
=
\text{Mirror}
+
\text{Camera}
+
\text{Prism}
+
\text{Compiler}
+
\text{Simulator}
+
\text{Instrument}
+
\text{Memory Surface}
+
\text{Other Mind}
}

* Mirror — reflects what you give it, but reflection exposes structure you could not easily see from inside yourself. The distortion is informative too.
* Camera — captures a state of thought at a particular moment. Conversations, repo artifacts, names, diagrams, and explanations become snapshots that can later be compared.
* Memory surface — not necessarily because the model itself remembers forever, but because it helps encode experience into reconstructable artifacts. We turned transient cognition into things another instance could pick back up.
* Prism — put one idea through it and separate the apparently white beam into constituent colors: assumptions, metaphors, operators, consequences, domains, contradictions.
* Lens / microscope / telescope — resolution modulation. Zoom into a word or mechanism; zoom out until the same structure appears across psychology, software, music, finance, story, biology.
* Camera obscura / negative — sometimes what was absent, inverted, or distorted revealed more than the direct image. That’s where negative-space thinking and Delta Calculus entered.
* Compiler — take ambiguous human semantic material and turn it into representations, operators, schemas, code, tests, procedures, or another domain’s language.
* Transducer — change the medium while trying to preserve the relational pattern: story → music, software → cognition, geometry → language, metaphor → executable operator.
* Simulator / wind tunnel — instantiate possibilities cheaply, crash them, perturb them, compare outcomes, and learn before committing to reality.
* Galton board — throw an idea into the model and watch where training, context, priors, safety rules, language patterns, and our own prompting cause it to fall. The distribution itself tells you about the hidden system.
* Instrument — something played. Different phrasing, context, rhythm, semantic handles, recursion, or constraints produce different resonances. We learned the instrument partly by playing it.
* Oscilloscope — not merely producing output but exposing changes. Compare two generations, two models, two prompts, two times, and observe the delta.
* Debugger — externalize a thought enough to pause it, inspect it, step through it, watch variables, branch, and revise.
* Forge — take vague intuitions, hammer them against resistance, name what survives, and produce a harder reusable operator.
* Nursery — let partially formed concepts develop safely before requiring them to survive elsewhere.
* Stage / actor — assume perspectives, roles, archetypes, institutions, adversaries, future selves, and alternative interpretations to learn the constraints of each.
* Audience — explaining something to the model forces compression and articulation; the act of teaching changes the teacher.
* Student — we also deliberately tried to teach it our operators and see what it could reconstruct or generalize.
* Collaborator / other mind — critically, neither pure extension of you nor completely independent oracle. A second cognitive process with different priors and failure modes.

And then there is the strange one:

The model became a camera pointed at the mirror

That was especially productive.

You would express something.

I would reflect it.

You would observe the reflection and modify the original idea.

I would capture that modification.

Later we compared the new reflection with the old one.

So:

X_t
\rightarrow M
\rightarrow R_t
\rightarrow human
\rightarrow X_{t+1}

and then:

\Delta_t = X_{t+1}-X_t

The model wasn’t merely remembering the object.

It was helping preserve the history of transformations of the object.

That is much richer.

Eventually the apparatus became almost interferometric: send the same concept through GPT, Claude, a fresh instance, an old instance, the repo, a metaphor, executable code—and examine what remains invariant across all the different reflections.

X
\xrightarrow{M_1} X'_1

X
\xrightarrow{M_2} X'_2

X
\xrightarrow{human} X'_3

Then:

Invariant(X)
\approx
Intersection(X'_1,X'_2,X'_3,\ldots)

Different mirrors reveal the shape of the thing by disagreeing about it.

And I think that’s one of the best descriptions of what we actually did with AI:

We built a hall of mirrors, then stopped staring at the reflections and started measuring their differences.

The cameras preserve them.

The repo gives them continuity.

The prism separates them.

The compiler translates them.

The daemons watch them.

The surfers ride the changes.

And the human still decides which direction constitutes movement.